# Day 2 {.section-slide}

::: {.notes}
Welcome back. Yesterday: DAGs, d-separation, do-operator, the Causal Quartet.
Today: what happens when the effect isn't the same for everyone?
:::

## Yesterday's punchline {.center}

$$
\begin{aligned}
ATE &= \mathbb{E}[Y_i(1) - Y_i(0)] \\
    &= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(X = 1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(X = 0)]
\end{aligned}
$$

::: {.fragment}
One number. For everyone.
:::

::: {.fragment style="margin-top:1.5em;"}
[**Is that enough?**]{style="background:var(--iqb-accent); color:white; padding:0.3em 1.4em; border-radius:6px; font-size:1.3em;"}
:::

::: {.notes}
Recap the ATE from day 1. One number summarizing the treatment effect across the entire population.
Pause. Let the question hang.
Yesterday we learned WHEN we can estimate it (identification). Today: what it hides.
:::

## {.center}

$$ATE = 0.1$$

::: {.fragment style="margin-top:1.5em;"}
The individual treatment effect $\tau_i = Y_i(1) - Y_i(0)$ is **unobserved**.
:::

::: {.fragment style="margin-top:1em;"}
What patterns of individual effects could hide behind this number?
:::

::: {.notes}
Open question to the room. Wait for answers.
Prompt if needed: "Does everyone benefit equally? Could some people be harmed?"
Collect 2-3 answers. Don't resolve — advance to the quartet.
:::

## {background-image="images/plots/gelman-quartet-variation.png" background-size="contain-padded-6"}

::: {.notes}
Reveal: four panels, all with ATE = 0.1 (dashed line).
Each dot is one person's true individual causal effect — the thing we can't observe.
Top-left: Constant — everyone gets ~0.1. The implicit default.
Top-right: Low variation — always positive, mild spread. Plausible.
Bottom-left: High variation — some people are HARMED. Average hides conflict.
Bottom-right: Occasional large effects — zero for most, huge for a few. Minority drives ATE.
Walk through each. "Which feels most realistic for an educational intervention?"
Gelman, Hullman & Kennedy (2023).
:::

## {.center}

Same average. Four different realities.

::: {.fragment style="margin-top:1.5em;"}
In **high variation**, some students are harmed.

In **occasional large effects**, most students get nothing.
:::

::: {.fragment style="margin-top:1em;"}
[**The ATE doesn't tell you which world you're in.**]{style="background:var(--iqb-accent); color:white; padding:0.3em 1.4em; border-radius:6px; font-size:1.1em;"}
:::

::: {.notes}
Let this land. The ATE is not wrong — it's incomplete.
Can we do better? If the effect varies systematically with something
we can measure, we can describe HOW it varies. That's what we formalize next.
:::

## Yesterday's toolkit {.center}

$$
\begin{aligned}
ATE &= \mathbb{E}[Y_i(1) - Y_i(0)] \\
    &= \mathbb{E}[f(1, U_i) - f(0, U_i)] \\
    &= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(X = 1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(X = 0)] \\
    &= \sum_z \left(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid X = 1, Z = z] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid X = 0, Z = z]\right) P(Z = z)
\end{aligned}
$$

::: {.notes}
Quick recap of all the ways to write the same number.
Line 1: Counterfactual / potential outcomes (Rubin).
Line 2: Structural — plug treatment into the SCM with each person's noise.
Line 3: Interventional — Pearl's do-operator.
Line 4: Adjustment — what you actually compute from data, given a valid adjustment set Z.
All four say the same thing. Yesterday we learned when line 4 is valid (backdoor criterion).
Today: what this single number hides.
:::

# DAGs and Functional Form {.section-slide}

::: {.notes}
Before we answer that question, a crucial distinction we glossed over yesterday.
DAGs encode WHICH variables cause WHICH. They say nothing about HOW.
:::

## An SCM Example

::::: {.columns}
:::: {.column width="50%"}

### Structural Equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
Z &\leftarrow f_Z(U_Z) \\
X &\leftarrow f_X(Z, U_X) \\
Y &\leftarrow f_Y(Z, X, U_Y)
\end{aligned}
$$

### Distributional Assumptions

$$
U_Z, U_X, U_Y \overset{\text{ind.}}{\sim} P(U)
$$

::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
![](images/dags/scm-example-full.svg){.absolute top="30" right="5" height="300"}
::::
:::::

## Graph Surgery

::::: {.columns}
:::: {.column width="50%"}

### Structural Equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
Z &\leftarrow f_Z(U_Z) \\
X &\leftarrow \color{#107895}{x} \\
Y &\leftarrow f_Y(Z, \textcolor{#107895}{x}, U_Y)
\end{aligned}
$$

### Distributional Assumptions

$$
U_Z, \textcolor{lightgray}{U_X}, U_Y \overset{\text{ind.}}{\sim} P(U)
$$

::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
![](images/dags/scm-example-do-x.svg){.absolute top="30" right="5" height="300"}
::::
:::::

## A Linear SCM

::::: {.columns}
:::: {.column width="50%"}

### Structural Equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
Z &= U_Z \\
X &= \beta^{XZ} Z + U_X \\
Y &= \beta^{YX} X + \beta^{YZ} Z + U_Y
\end{aligned}
$$

### Distributional Assumptions

$$
\begin{pmatrix} U_Z \\ U_X \\ U_Y \end{pmatrix} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} \sigma^2_Z & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \sigma^2_X & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \sigma^2_Y \end{pmatrix}\right)
$$
::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
![](images/dags/scm-example-full.svg){.absolute top="30" right="5" height="300"}
::::
:::::

## Graph Surgery: Linear Case

::::: {.columns}
:::: {.column width="50%"}

### Structural Equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
Z &= U_Z \\
X &= \color{#107895}{x} \\
Y &= \beta^{YX} \textcolor{#107895}{x} + \beta^{YZ} Z + U_Y
\end{aligned}
$$

### Distributional Assumptions

$$
\begin{pmatrix} U_Z \\ \textcolor{lightgray}{U_X} \\ U_Y \end{pmatrix} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \textcolor{lightgray}{0} \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} \sigma^2_Z & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \textcolor{lightgray}{\sigma^2_X} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \sigma^2_Y \end{pmatrix}\right)
$$
::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
![](images/dags/scm-example-do-x.svg){.absolute top="30" right="5" height="300"}
::::
:::::

## {background-image="images/plots/fork-parallel-slopes.png" background-size="contain-padded-6"}

::: {.notes}
This is what conditioning on Z looks like visually.
Color = Z value. Each Z-slice has its own regression line.
All three lines have the same slope — the causal effect is the same everywhere.
The dotted line is the naive (confounded) aggregate from before.
Conditioning removed the bias. The effect is homogeneous.
Now: what if the effect ISN'T the same everywhere?
:::

## A Linear SCM with Interaction

::::: {.columns}
:::: {.column width="50%"}

### Structural Equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
Z &= U_Z \\
X &= \beta^{XZ} Z + U_X \\
Y &= \beta^{YX} X + \beta^{YZ} Z + \textcolor{#D55E00}{\beta^{YXZ} XZ} + U_Y
\end{aligned}
$$

### Distributional Assumptions

$$
\begin{pmatrix} U_Z \\ U_X \\ U_Y \end{pmatrix} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} \sigma^2_Z & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \sigma^2_X & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \sigma^2_Y \end{pmatrix}\right)
$$
::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
![](images/dags/scm-example-full.svg){.absolute top="30" right="5" height="300"}
::::
:::::

## Graph Surgery: Interaction Case

::::: {.columns}
:::: {.column width="50%"}

### Structural Equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
Z &= U_Z \\
X &= \color{#107895}{x} \\
Y &= \beta^{YX} \textcolor{#107895}{x} + \beta^{YZ} Z + \textcolor{#D55E00}{\beta^{YXZ} \textcolor{#107895}{x} Z} + U_Y
\end{aligned}
$$

### Distributional Assumptions

$$
\begin{pmatrix} U_Z \\ \textcolor{lightgray}{U_X} \\ U_Y \end{pmatrix} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \textcolor{lightgray}{0} \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} \sigma^2_Z & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \textcolor{lightgray}{\sigma^2_X} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & \sigma^2_Y \end{pmatrix}\right)
$$
::::

:::: {.column width="50%"}
![](images/dags/scm-example-do-x.svg){.absolute top="30" right="5" height="300"}
::::
:::::

## {background-image="images/plots/interaction-cate-slopes.png" background-size="contain-padded-6"}

::: {.notes}
Same visual technique — color by Z, look within strata.
But now the lines AREN'T parallel. They fan out.
Low Z (purple): flat or negative slope. High Z (yellow): steep positive slope.
The dashed line is the average — the ATE. It hides the variation.
Same DAG. Same do-operator. But the answer depends on Z.
:::

# Heterogeneity {.section-slide}

::: {.notes}
The effect can vary — and the DAG alone won't tell us.
Effects vary across people, across groups, across time.
The ATE averages over all of that. Sometimes that's fine. Sometimes it's misleading.
:::

## {.center}

$$
\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(X = x), Z = z] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(X = x'), Z = z]
$$

::: {.fragment}
$$= \textcolor{#107895}{\beta^{YX}} + \textcolor{#D55E00}{\beta^{YXZ}} z$$
:::

::: {.fragment style="margin-top:1.5em;"}

| | Estimand | Value |
|---|---|---|
| **ATE** | Average over all $Z$ | $\textcolor{#107895}{\beta^{YX}}$ |
| **CATE** | At a specific $Z = z$ | $\textcolor{#107895}{\beta^{YX}} + \textcolor{#D55E00}{\beta^{YXZ}} z$ |

:::

::: {.notes}
Now we can name what we saw. The CATE keeps Z visible instead of averaging over it.
The ATE is the average of all CATEs, weighted by the distribution of Z.
At Z = 0, they coincide. Away from zero, they diverge.
The ATE of β^YX hides the fact that some people are harmed and others helped a lot.
:::

## {background-image="images/plots/gelman-quartet-heterogeneous.png" background-size="contain-padded-6"}

::: {.notes}
Earlier we saw the same ATE hiding four patterns of individual effects — random variation around the average.
Now the variation is systematic. The causal effect changes as a function of a covariate — that's exactly the CATE we just defined.
Top-left: Linear interaction — this IS the β^YX + β^YXZ·z model we just worked through. The line we drew.
Top-right: No effect then increase — treatment only kicks in above a threshold. A linear model would miss this.
Bottom-left: Plateau — effect saturates, ceiling on what treatment can do.
Bottom-right: Sweet spot — only works for intermediate values. Nothing at extremes.
All four produce the same ATE. A linear interaction model would fit only the first.
"Which shape best describes your intervention?"
Gelman, Hullman & Kennedy (2023).
:::

## {background-image="images/plots/categorical-cate-schools.png" background-size="contain-padded-6"}

::: {.notes}
So far Z was continuous. But the most common source of heterogeneity in your data isn't continuous — it's grouping.
Students in schools. Each school gets its own regression line — its own effect of X on Y.
Some steep, some flat, one slightly negative. The dashed line is the ATE — the average across all schools.
This is the same fanning-slopes pattern, just discrete.
:::

## {.center}

| | Estimand | Averages over |
|---|---|---|---|
| **ATE** | $= E[\beta_j]$ | All schools | School differences |
| **CATE(j)** | $= \beta_j$ | School $j$ | Nothing — school-specific |

::: {.notes}
Same framework as before. The ATE averages over all schools.
The CATE keeps the school visible. Some schools show strong effects, others nothing.
The ATE hides that — same story as continuous Z, just discrete.
:::

# Multilevel Models {background-color="#427083"}

##

