![](day2-advanced_files/figure-revealjs/bflpe-homogeneous-1.png){width=960}
:::
:::


[Illustration of the Big Fish Little Pond Effect based on simulated data, cf. @marshBigfishlittlepondeffectStandsCritical2008]

::: {.notes}
- **Core idea**: A student’s academic self-concept (how smart they feel) depends not just on their actual ability, but on the ability of their peers.
- If you’re a big fish in a little pond — say, the top student in an average school — you’ll usually have a higher academic self-concept than if you’re the same student but placed in a very selective, high-achieving school (where suddenly you’re a little fish in a big pond).

**Between:**

- Selective school (higher performance) -> lower academic self esteem
- Public school (lower performance) -> higher self esteem

-> *negative relationship*


**Within:**

- comparison to peers: higher performance -> higher academic self esteem

-> *positive relationship*
:::

